<figure class="deadline">
  <figcaption><span>Figure 3</span> Leave time for the answer</figcaption>
  <div class="budget"><b>Example request allowance: 200 ms</b><span>The caller supplies its remaining deadline.</span></div>
  <div class="path"><div class="read"><b>GET</b><span>≤ 25 ms</span><small>Pool acquisition, connection and reply</small></div><div class="source"><b>Source read</b><span>Remaining mandatory time</span><small>Only after source admission</small></div><div class="fill"><b>Optional SET</b><span>≤ 20 ms</span><small>Less if the deadline is close</small></div><div class="return"><b>Return</b><span>Keep 5 ms</span><small>Skip SET if no allowance remains</small></div></div>
  <div class="outcomes"><p><b>GET uses its 25 ms:</b> record the timeout, then try the bounded source path.</p><p><b>Source reaches the deadline:</b> propagate the timeout; there is no result to save.</p><p><b>SET uses its allowance:</b> stop waiting and return the source result already obtained.</p></div>
  <p class="caption">Illustrative limits from the adapter, not measured latencies. The boxes show responsibilities, not a time scale. Connection setup and socket reads each have a 20 ms client limit; the 25 ms wrapper caps their combined GET wait. Event-loop cancellation is cooperative.</p>
</figure>
